<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jadeja</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jadeja Rajputs</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Jadeja are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> clan who ruled Kutch from the 16th century and spread into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>. In 1540 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jam-rawal">Jam Rawal</a> founded Nawanagar, and other branches held Rajkot, Gondal, Morvi, Malia, Kotda Sangani and many smaller states. The Jadeja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> lasted until they acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947–1948.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jadeja.jpg" alt="Jadeja" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mrs Postan 1838 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jadeja_Chief.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Jadeja live mainly in the Kutch district of the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and in the Tharparkar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. They originated among the Sammas of Sindh, a pastoral people. Oral tradition dates their rise to the late 9th century, when Lakho Ghuraro and Lakho Phulani, descendants of the clan's founder Jam Jada, set up kingdoms in parts of Kutch and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a>, but written sources place it in the 14th century. Historians such as Anisha Saxena and Rushbrooke see them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> branches of the Samma dynasty, whose leaders took the title of Jam, while the sociologist Lyla Mehta describes them as Hindu descendants of a Muslim tribe that had moved from Sindh to Kutch.</p><p>Through marriages with Sodha Rajput women, a process called Rajputisation, they claimed Rajput identity, and once in power they modelled themselves on the Rajputs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>. They claim descent from Jamshid of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>, claims historians regard as without historical basis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Jadeji Rani Kamabai, sister of Jam Khengarji I of Kutch, was married to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, who raised Khengar to the title of Rao. Khengarji I formed the princely state of Kutch by gathering under him twelve related Jadeja landowning families and two families of the Waghela tribe, the Bhayat, treated as brothers, whose allegiance he and his successors kept. Other Jadeja houses ruled Dhrol, Malia, Morvi, Rajkot, Nawanagar, Virpur and Gondal. Among their rulers Bhagvadsinhji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Gondal from 1869 to 1944, published the Bhagvadgomandal, and Khengarji III of Cutch represented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> in 1921.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Jadeja held high social status and forbade marriage both with lower groups and within the clan, which made suitable matches for daughters hard to find and dowries costly. The clan developed a tradition of female infanticide; when the British outlawed it, Jadeja chiefs began to marry their daughters to other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> chiefs of equal status. The custom of ojjal kept Jadeja women out of public view, so that Lyla Mehta found Jadeja men, unusually, fetching water from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> in Kutch. The principal deity of the clan is Ashapura Mata, the Hope-Giving Mother.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jadeja” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Jadeja Rajputs</b> came from Sindh and settled in Kutch in the medieval period; the register's date reflects tradition rather than firm record.</blockquote><a href="/state/cutch-state">Cutch State</a></li><li><blockquote>It belonged to the wider family of <b>Jadeja</b> states that spread from Kutch into northern Kathiawar.</blockquote><a href="/state/dhrol-state">Dhrol State</a></li><li><blockquote>Gondal was founded in 1634 by Kumbhoji I, a member of the <b>Jadeja</b> house of Rajkot, who received the area as his share.</blockquote><a href="/state/gondal-state">Gondal State</a></li><li><blockquote>Kotda Sangani was held by a <b>Jadeja Rajput</b> family related to the ruling house of Gondal.</blockquote><a href="/state/kotda-sangani-state">Kotda Sangani State</a></li><li><blockquote>Kotharia was held by a branch of the <b>Jadeja Rajputs</b> of Rajkot, who granted lands to younger sons as their share.</blockquote><a href="/state/kotharia-state">Kotharia State</a></li><li><blockquote>Malia was carved out around 1720 by a cadet of the <b>Jadeja Rajput</b> family that ruled Morvi, itself an offshoot of the ruling house of Cutch.</blockquote><a href="/state/malia-state">Malia State</a></li></ul></section>
